# Benchmark claims

The benchmark policy was written before Adze produced a single number, so that it
could not be adjusted to fit one. Treat it as a constraint on what may be
published, not as guidance.

Full reasoning: `docs/architecture/adr/0011-benchmark-harness.md`. Operational
version: `docs/benchmarks/strategy.md`.

## The two rules that cost us headlines

- **No win claimed inside 3 percentage points.** Published work on infrastructure
  noise in agent evaluation measured a 6-point gap (p < 0.01) between the
  most- and least-resourced container configurations — larger than the gap
  between top leaderboard models. Naive binomial intervals already span 1–2
  points and infra confounders stack on top. A 2-point lead is not a win, and we
  say so even when the 2 points are ours.
- **No aggregator citations, ever.** A cluster of SEO leaderboard sites was found
  publishing 96%, 96.4%, and 97.0% for the same model on the same benchmark whose
  official leaderboard topped out at 79.20%. Acceptable sources are first-party
  harnesses and leaderboards, and named independent evaluators with published
  methodology. One polluted citation discredits an otherwise sound report.

## Statistics

- **`pass@1` is the metric.** Report **mean ± SEM over ≥3 attempts**. Never
  max-over-N. Best-of-N is a separate, explicitly labelled mode and never a
  headline number.
- Report seeds and attempt count. A single-run number is not a result.
- Report cost and tokens split **input / cached-input / output, with cache hit
  rate**. Cache economics move effective cost by more than 10×, so omitting the
  split makes a cost claim meaningless.
- `solves per million completion tokens` is a headline metric, not a footnote.
  The competitive claim is on the joint accuracy-and-cost curve.

## Reporting format

Reports go in `bench/reports/<date>-<suite>/` with `report.md`, `result.json`,
`config.json`, `trajectories/`, and `audit.md`.

- **`report.md` leads with limitations, not with the headline number.** If the
  code writes the report, the limitations section is emitted first — this is a
  property of the generator, not of the author's discipline.
- **Publish trajectories for every trial, failures included.** Publishing
  failures is the strongest available credibility signal, and a report that only
  contains passes is not checkable.
- Include a negative-results section: regressions, infra error rate, timeout
  rate, malformed-edit rate.
- Include container digests, resource floor *and* ceiling, pinned dated model
  snapshots with effort level and temperature, the harness version tag, and the
  exact invocation.
- Include our own broken-task audit of the benchmark being reported on. Roughly a
  third of the "hardest" tasks in circulation are broken rather than hard.
- The Tier-1 SWE-bench smoke slice is a wiring check. **Its number is never
  published.**

## Leakage assertions are build failures

Test-patch absence, gold-patch-field absence, future-git-history absence, and
network isolation are asserted in CI and fail the build. They are not warnings and
not a checklist item someone remembers. The two-container design — the agent
commits, and only the diff crosses to a fresh verifier — is what makes most of
these structural rather than policed.

## Honesty about what a suite actually measures

- Never describe a planned suite as producing numbers. `nep-bench` and
  `index-bench` are roadmap items (M6); say so.
- State the input distribution plainly. A suite run on synthetic edits measures
  the applier, not model behavior, and the report must say which one it measured.
- Do not describe a metric as "per model" when only one synthetic source of
  inputs exists.

## Isolation from product code

Nothing under `bench/` may be imported by product code. Benchmark code must not
influence what ships, or the benchmark stops measuring the product.
